// prism_inspect -- the TREE INSPECTOR: pretty-printing, index-path addressing, kind/key search,
// a distinct-kinds summary, and a renderable inspector view over a built PrismNode tree.
// Milestone MB.11 (the unfinished half -- MB.11's ANSI `cols` wrapping and the MB.11x import-surface
// lesson landed first; this closes the milestone).
//
// ── WHY THIS EXISTS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `prism_obs_stats` (MB.10, obs/prism_obs.nova) answers "what does this tree COST" -- total nodes,
// depth, a count per kind. It does NOT answer "where, in a tree of hundreds of nodes, is the ONE
// node I'm looking for" or "show me the shape of this subtree so I can actually read it." The MB.6
// docs page alone is 571 nodes; MB.10's own measurement of it is 573 nodes at depth 9 -- printing
// that whole tree unindented, or hunting through it by eye for "which node is the third `press`",
// is not a realistic debugging workflow. This file is the difference between "the tree has a
// problem" (obs) and "here is the exact node, at this exact path, that has the problem" (inspect).
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: PATH ADDRESSING IS INDEX-BASED, NOT KEY-BASED ────────────────────────────
// `key: string?` (PRISM_SPEC.md §12.6) exists ONLY on rows built through `prism_node_keyed` --
// typically `each`/`grid` children -- and is `none()` on every other node in a real tree (a
// `stack`, a `label`, a `press`, ...). A key-based address ("go to the row keyed 'row-42'") cannot
// name an unkeyed node at all, so it cannot be the GENERAL addressing scheme this file needs --
// `prism_inspect_at([])` must be able to reach any node in any tree, keyed or not. Every node,
// keyed or not, DOES have a stable position among its parent's children in source order (the same
// invariant `prism_obs_diff`'s positional path already leans on for unkeyed containers). An index
// path is therefore the only addressing scheme complete over the whole vocabulary; find_key (below)
// is offered as a second, narrower lookup for the keyed case specifically, returning index paths
// too, so both search modes compose with the one `prism_inspect_at` navigator.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: FIND_KIND / FIND_KEY HAND-ROLL THEIR OWN RECURSION ───────────────────────
// `prism_node_walk` (prism_node.nova) is a pre-order visitor with no path context -- `visit(n)`
// sees only the node, never how it was reached. Finding a node's ADDRESS needs exactly the context
// that visitor cannot supply, the identical reason `prism_obs_diff` (MB.10) hand-writes its own
// recursion instead of using `prism_node_walk` for the diff. `prism_inspect_summary`, by contrast,
// only needs "is a descendant" (collecting distinct kind names), which is exactly what
// `prism_node_walk`'s visitor gives for free -- so it uses the walk, and find_kind/find_key do not,
// for the same reason MB.10 made that split.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: TRUNCATION IS BYTE-LENGTH, NOT DISPLAY-CELL-WIDTH ────────────────────────
// `text/prism_text.nova` (MB.10) already has a UTF-8-cell-width-aware `prism_text_truncate`. This
// file does NOT import it. Two independent reasons, not one:
//   1. IMPORT SURFACE: importing `prism_text` pulls in its own transitive imports, and MB.11x
//      (this same milestone) found EXACTLY this shape of bug -- widening a backend's import surface
//      through prism_text made every label render empty, because NOVA's field-name -> slot table is
//      GLOBAL and last-registration-wins. The fix there was "keep the backend's import surface at
//      two modules"; the identical discipline applies here. This file's import surface is the three
//      modules the milestone brief specifies (prism_node, prism_content, prism_arrange) and nothing
//      wider -- a dev tool is exactly the kind of file that gets imported by other dev tools and
//      test harnesses, so a slot collision introduced HERE would propagate to whoever pulls this in.
//   2. HONESTY OF PURPOSE: this is a DEV tool, read in a terminal or a log by the person who wrote
//      the tree, not a user-facing render. Cutting a multi-byte UTF-8 character in half mid-codepoint
//      is a real correctness bug for anything a real user reads (MB.10/11's whole point); for a
//      debug line that says `text="café...`, a byte-exact cut is legible enough and costs zero
//      import surface. This is a stated tradeoff, not an oversight -- the day this inspector needs
//      to render CJK/emoji-heavy trees legibly is the day to reconsider, not before.
// Truncation is proven correct here (never emits a line longer than `max_width`, and the KAT proves
// it directly against a hostile long label at a small width) rather than merely asserted.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: DEPTH/COUNT ON BRANCH LINES, ATTR VALUE ON LEAF LINES, KEY ON EITHER ─────
// A branch node's `attrs` are rarely the interesting thing about it (a `stack`/`band`/`grid` carries
// structural attrs like `cols`, not a caption) -- what a reader of a tree dump wants at a branch is
// its SIZE (`prism_node_depth`/`prism_node_count`, already cached at construction -- see
// prism_node.nova's header -- so reading them here is O(1), not a re-walk). A leaf's `text`/`source`
// IS the interesting thing about it, and is shown instead. `key` is orthogonal to both (a branch
// row from `each`/`grid` -- e.g. a keyed `band` -- is exactly as printable a case as a keyed leaf),
// so it is checked independently and prefixed onto whichever of the other two forms applies.
//
// ── HONEST LIMITATION: INDENT IS NOT CLAMPED AT PATHOLOGICAL DEPTH ────────────────────────────
// PRISM_SPEC.md §16 allows tree depth up to 256; at 2 spaces/level that is 512 columns of pure
// indent before a single character of content, which could exceed a small `max_width` on its own.
// The MB.11 ANSI backend clamps its own indent to a floor of 8 cells for exactly this reason. This
// file does NOT reproduce that clamp: a depth-256 tree is an adversarial-fuzzer shape, not a debugging
// session's real tree, and a caller who passes an unrealistically small `max_width` to a tool
// gets a dev tool that told the truth about an unindentable tree rather than a clamp silently
// second-guessing the request. Stated, not silently guessed around.
// The SAME reasoning covers a branch line's `(depth=N, count=N)` suffix: it is never truncated,
// because it isn't an attr VALUE (the thing the milestone brief asks this file to truncate) -- it
// is bounded structural metadata (N <= 256 for depth, N <= 100000 for count, PRISM_SPEC.md §16's own
// ceilings, so at most 9 digits total) that would become misleading rather than merely shorter if
// cut. In practice `kind name + " (depth=256, count=100000)"` is under 40 bytes even at the
// vocabulary's longest kind name and both ceilings simultaneously, so this is a real bound, not an
// unbounded risk -- just not one this file's truncation machinery (built for free-form string attr
// values) applies to.
//
// PUBLIC API
//   prism_inspect_pretty(node, max_width)     -> string           indented, depth/count on
//                                                                  branches, text/source/key on
//                                                                  leaves, values truncated to fit
//   prism_inspect_at(node, path: list<int>)   -> Result<PrismNode> navigate by child indices;
//                                                                  [] = root; out-of-range or
//                                                                  non-int path element -> typed Err
//   prism_inspect_find_kind(node, kind_name)  -> list             every index path to a node whose
//                                                                  kind_str == kind_name (pre-order)
//   prism_inspect_find_key(node, key_val)     -> list             every index path to a node whose
//                                                                  key == some(key_val)
//   prism_inspect_summary(node)               -> string           "kind: N nodes, depth D, K kinds
//                                                                  (a, b, c)" -- kinds sorted
//                                                                  alphabetically (insertion sort)
//   prism_inspect_tree(node)                  -> Result<PrismNode> the pretty-print, one line per
//                                                                  prism_label, in a prism_stack --
//                                                                  so the inspector's own output
//                                                                  renders through either backend

import prism_node
import prism_content
import prism_arrange

// ── Pretty-print ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// `level` spaces of indent, 2 per level. A plain accumulation loop -- no string-repeat operator or
// padding helper is pulled in from elsewhere (see header: import surface stays at exactly three
// modules), and this is cheap enough (at most 256 iterations, PRISM_MAX_TREE_DEPTH) not to need one.
fn _inspect_indent(level: int) -> string
    let mut s = ""
    let mut i = 0
    while i < level
        s = s + "  "
        i += 1
    s

// Cuts `value` to fit within `budget` BYTES, appending "..." when something was actually cut so a
// truncated value is never confused with a short one that happened to fit. `budget <= 0` means
// there is no room at all -- returns "" rather than a negative-length slice. `budget <= 3` means
// there isn't room for even one real content byte plus the full "..." marker together in a way that
// would show both usefully, so it falls back to the raw (still budget-bounded) prefix bytes with no
// marker -- a documented tie-break for an edge case, not a bug: at that width there is no shown form
// that is unambiguously better than another.
fn _inspect_truncate_byte(value: string, budget: int) -> string
    if budget <= 0
        return ""
    if len(value) <= budget
        return value
    if budget <= 3
        return slice(value, 0, budget)
    slice(value, 0, budget - 3) + "..."

// Appends ` name="VALUE"` to `line`, truncating VALUE so the FINAL line length never exceeds
// `max_width`. The budget math: this call adds exactly `len(label) + len(shown) + 1` bytes to
// `line` (label = ` name="`, the trailing `+1` is the closing quote), so the value's budget is
// whatever remains of `max_width` after `line`'s current length and that fixed overhead -- proven
// in the KAT by checking every emitted line's length directly against `max_width`, not merely
// checking that truncation "looks like it happened."
fn _inspect_append_attr(line: string, attr_name: string, raw_val: string, max_width: int) -> string
    let label = " " + attr_name + "=\""
    let overhead = len(line) + len(label) + 1
    let budget = max_width - overhead
    let shown = _inspect_truncate_byte(raw_val, budget)
    line + label + shown + "\""

// One node's own line, with NO trailing newline and NO recursion into children (that is
// `_inspect_collect_lines`'s job) -- kept separate so `prism_inspect_tree` and the KAT can each
// exercise exactly one node's rendering in isolation without building a whole subtree first.
fn _inspect_render_line(n: PrismNode, level: int, max_width: int) -> string
    let indent = _inspect_indent(level)
    let mut line = indent + prism_node.prism_node_kind_str(n)
    if prism_node.prism_node_has_key(n)
        line = _inspect_append_attr(line, "key", _inspect_key_of(n), max_width)
    let child_count = prism_node.prism_node_child_count(n)
    if child_count > 0
        line = line + " (depth=" + str(prism_node.prism_node_depth(n)) + ", count=" + str(prism_node.prism_node_count(n)) + ")"
    else
        if prism_node.prism_node_has_attr(n, "text")
            line = _inspect_append_attr(line, "text", prism_node.prism_node_attr_str(n, "text", ""), max_width)
        else if prism_node.prism_node_has_attr(n, "source")
            line = _inspect_append_attr(line, "source", prism_node.prism_node_attr_str(n, "source", ""), max_width)
    line

// `n.key` defaulted to "" for None, mirroring the identical helper in obs/prism_obs.nova
// (`_prism_obs_key_of`) -- not imported from there (import surface, see header), rewritten locally.
fn _inspect_key_of(n: PrismNode) -> string
    match prism_node.prism_node_key(n)
        Some(k) => k
        None => ""

// Pre-order: this node's own line, then every child's lines at `level + 1`. Children of an
// already-built `PrismNode` are guaranteed to themselves be valid `PrismNode`s (prism_node_new
// validates every child at construction -- see that file's header on why its OWN loop needs the
// `type_name` guard: it is validating a FRESH, caller-supplied list before this invariant exists
// yet). Reading them here needs no re-guard, the same reasoning prism_obs.nova's diff functions
// rely on when indexing an already-built tree's children directly.
fn _inspect_collect_lines(n: PrismNode, level: int, max_width: int, out: list)
    out.push(_inspect_render_line(n, level, max_width))
    for c in prism_node.prism_node_children(n)
        _inspect_collect_lines(c, level + 1, max_width, out)

fn prism_inspect_pretty(node: PrismNode, max_width: int) -> string
    let lines = []
    _inspect_collect_lines(node, 0, max_width, lines)
    join(lines, "\n")

// ── Path addressing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Navigates `path` (a list of child indices) from `node`. `[]` returns `node` itself. Every element
// is validated BEFORE use: a non-int element (a hostile/malformed path, e.g. built by hand rather
// than returned from `prism_inspect_find_kind`) and an out-of-range index are both a typed `Err`
// naming the exact path position and the actual child count -- never a crash and never a silent
// clamp, matching PRISM_SPEC.md §16's "a typed error... never a crash" standard applied here to a
// dev tool's own input rather than to tree construction.
fn prism_inspect_at(node: PrismNode, path: list) -> Result<PrismNode>
    let mut current = node
    for idx, step in path
        if type_name(step) != "int"
            return err("prism_inspect_at: path[" + str(idx) + "] is not an int (got " + type_name(step) + ")")
        let kids = prism_node.prism_node_children(current)
        let n_kids = len(kids)
        if step < 0 or step >= n_kids
            return err("prism_inspect_at: path[" + str(idx) + "] = " + str(step) + " is out of range (node has " + str(n_kids) + " children)")
        // Guard before the field read the next loop iteration (or the final `ok`) performs on
        // `current` -- see prism_node.nova's header on why an untyped list element needs a
        // `type_name` check before it is trusted as a PrismNode. A real tree's children are always
        // valid PrismNodes (prism_node_new enforces it at construction), so this should never fire;
        // it exists as the same defensive chokepoint prism_node_new itself uses, not because a
        // failure is expected here.
        let candidate = kids[step]
        if type_name(candidate) != "PrismNode"
            return err("prism_inspect_at: child at path[" + str(idx) + "] is not a PrismNode (got " + type_name(candidate) + ") -- corrupt tree")
        current = candidate
    ok(current)

// ── Find by kind / key ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn _inspect_walk_kind(n: PrismNode, path: list, kind_name: string, out: list)
    if prism_node.prism_node_kind_str(n) == kind_name
        out.push(path)
    for idx, c in prism_node.prism_node_children(n)
        _inspect_walk_kind(c, path + [idx], kind_name, out)

// Pre-order paths to every node whose kind matches `kind_name`. `[]` in the result set means the
// ROOT itself matched. Order is deterministic (source child order, same as every other walk in
// this codebase -- PRISM_SPEC.md §15.9).
fn prism_inspect_find_kind(node: PrismNode, kind_name: string) -> list
    let out = []
    _inspect_walk_kind(node, [], kind_name, out)
    out

fn _inspect_walk_key(n: PrismNode, path: list, key_val: string, out: list)
    if prism_node.prism_node_has_key(n) and _inspect_key_of(n) == key_val
        out.push(path)
    for idx, c in prism_node.prism_node_children(n)
        _inspect_walk_key(c, path + [idx], key_val, out)

// Same shape as `prism_inspect_find_kind`, matching on `key == some(key_val)` instead of kind. An
// unkeyed node can never match (its key is `none()`), so this quietly returns fewer results on a
// tree with no keyed rows rather than treating "no key" as a match for `key_val == ""`.
fn prism_inspect_find_key(node: PrismNode, key_val: string) -> list
    let out = []
    _inspect_walk_key(node, [], key_val, out)
    out

// ── Summary ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn _inspect_note_kind(n: PrismNode, seen: dict)
    seen[prism_node.prism_node_kind_str(n)] = true

// Plain insertion sort over strings (ascending, lexicographic `<`/`>`), matching the exact idiom
// `forge_divisors.nova`/`forge_meetingrooms.nova` already use for ints -- verified directly (a
// throwaway probe) that NOVA's `<`/`>` on two strings compares lexicographically before relying on
// it here. At most 22 distinct kind names can ever appear (PrismNodeKind's whole closed vocabulary),
// so O(n^2) insertion sort costs nothing real; a hand-rolled sort also keeps this file's import
// surface at its three modules rather than pulling in std/core/sort for a 22-element bound.
fn _inspect_sort_strings(names: list) -> list
    let out = []
    for n in names
        out.push(n)
    let len_n = len(out)
    let mut i = 1
    while i < len_n
        let key = out[i]
        let mut j = i - 1
        while j >= 0 and out[j] > key
            out[j + 1] = out[j]
            j -= 1
        out[j + 1] = key
        i += 1
    out

// One line: root kind, total node count and max depth (both O(1) -- cached at construction, see
// prism_node.nova's header), and the DISTINCT kind names present anywhere in the tree, sorted
// alphabetically. "kind" text is collected via `prism_node_walk` (needs no path context, so unlike
// find_kind/find_key above this reuses the shared visitor rather than hand-rolling recursion again
// -- see header design note on which of the two patterns applies where).
fn prism_inspect_summary(node: PrismNode) -> string
    let seen = {}
    prism_node.prism_node_walk(node, fn(n) _inspect_note_kind(n, seen))
    let names = []
    for kind_name, present in seen
        names.push(kind_name)
    let sorted_names = _inspect_sort_strings(names)
    let root_kind = prism_node.prism_node_kind_str(node)
    let total = prism_node.prism_node_count(node)
    let depth = prism_node.prism_node_depth(node)
    root_kind + ": " + str(total) + " nodes, depth " + str(depth) + ", " + str(len(sorted_names)) + " kinds (" + join(sorted_names, ", ") + ")"

// ── Inspector as a renderable tree ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The width used internally for the pretty-print this renders -- wide enough that a realistic tree
// (short labels, a few attrs) rarely truncates, without being so wide that a rendered inspector page
// itself becomes unreadable. `prism_inspect_pretty` remains the entry point for a caller who wants
// their OWN width; this constant is this function's own opinion about "a reasonable default,"
// exactly the same role MB.6's dashboard/docs pages pick their own reasonable defaults for things
// PRISM_SPEC.md leaves to the caller.
fn _inspect_tree_default_width() -> int
    100

// Renders `prism_inspect_pretty`'s output as a node tree: one `prism_label` per line, in a
// `prism_stack` -- so the inspector's own output is itself a `PrismNode` and can be handed to
// EITHER real backend (HTML or ANSI) exactly like any other page, rather than needing its own
// private rendering path. Mirrors `prism_catalog_tree`'s own reasoning (dev/prism_catalog.nova):
// a dev tool that is itself built from real primitives cannot silently drift from what those
// primitives actually do.
fn prism_inspect_tree(node: PrismNode) -> Result<PrismNode>
    let text = prism_inspect_pretty(node, _inspect_tree_default_width())
    let raw_lines = split(text, "\n")
    let labels = []
    for ln in raw_lines
        let lbl = prism_content.prism_label(ln)
        if is_err(lbl)
            return err(unwrap_err(lbl))
        labels.push(unwrap(lbl))
    prism_arrange.prism_stack(labels)
